<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prohibition in the United States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prohibition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was the nationwide ban on the production, import and sale of alcoholic drinks from 1920 to 1933, under the Eighteenth Amendment to the Constitution. It encouraged bootlegging, smuggling and organised crime, with rum runners operating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. It was repealed by the Twenty-first Amendment in December 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prohibition-in-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Prohibition in the United States" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detroit_police_inspecting_equipment_found_in_a_clandestine_underground_brewery_during_the_prohibition_era_-_NARA_-_541928.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Drinking had been contested in America since the colonial period. The American Temperance Society, formed in 1826, had reached 1.5 million members by 1835. The dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> continued from the 1840s, led by Protestant denominations, and in the late 19th century it linked saloons with political corruption. Maine's law of 1851 banned the manufacture and sale of liquor, and twelve states followed before its repeal in 1856. The Woman's Christian Temperance Union gave the movement a national grassroots base, and after 1900 the Anti-Saloon League coordinated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Senate proposed the Eighteenth Amendment on 18 December 1917, and it became part of the Constitution when a 36th state approved it on 16 January 1919; the country went dry on 17 January 1920. The Volstead Act set the rules for enforcement and defined the banned drinks. Religious use of wine was permitted, and private possession and consumption were not illegal under federal law, though many local laws were stricter. Enforcement lacked a central authority, some states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> refused to enforce the amendment, and the terrain, ports and long borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> made smuggling hard to stop.</p><p>Many of the wealthy stockpiled liquor before 1920, while small portable stills were on sale within a week of the ban. Within six months over 15,000 doctors and 57,000 pharmacists held licences to prescribe or sell medicinal alcohol. A black market grew up, and organised crime has been argued to have gained a financial basis from it. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, the gang leader Al Capone operated despite the police.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Before the Volstead Act about 14% of federal, state and local tax revenue came from alcohol, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> struck, governments wanted that income. Captain William H. Stayton's Association Against the Prohibition Amendment, founded in 1918, was the largest of nearly forty groups working for repeal. The Twenty-first Amendment repealed the Eighteenth on 5 December 1933, the only time in American history that an amendment was passed to repeal another. It lets states restrict or ban alcohol, and dry counties remain.</p><p>Its effects are disputed. One review concluded that Prohibition probably reduced per capita alcohol use and harm at first, but that the benefits eroded as a black market grew. Rates of cirrhosis fell, while another study found consumption fell to about 30 percent of its earlier level and later rose to 60–70 percent. The historian Lisa McGirr argues that it expanded the powers of the federal state and fell hardest on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>, immigrants and poor whites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prohibition in the United States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wrecking, salt raking, sponging and later blockade running in the American Civil War and rum running during <b>Prohibition</b> sustained the economy.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bahamas">Colony of the Bahamas</a></li><li><blockquote>The decline of sugar and the end of <b>Prohibition</b> hurt the economy, which later turned to tourism, cruise shipping and, on Saint Croix, a large oil refinery that closed in 2012.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a></li></ul></section>
